/* READING - Complete the Words : banque x2 (cz001..cz120). diff : 2 = B2, 3 = C1, 4 = C2 */
(function () {
  CONTENT.ctw.push(
  // ---------- natural sciences ----------
  { id: 'cz001', diff: 2, topic: 'Tides', text: "Ocean tides are caused mainly by the gravitational pull of the Moon. The water on the side of the Earth facing the Moon is drawn toward it, which creates a bulge. A second bulge forms on the opposite side because of the way the Earth and Moon rotate around a common center. As the planet turns, coastlines pass through these bulges, so most places experience two high tides and two low tides each day." },
  { id: 'cz002', diff: 2, topic: 'Auroras', text: "Auroras are colorful displays of light that appear in the sky near the polar regions. They begin when charged particles from the Sun are carried toward the Earth by the solar wind. The planet's magnetic field guides these particles toward the poles, where they collide with gases in the upper atmosphere. Oxygen produces green and sometimes red light, while nitrogen creates blue and purple shades. Auroras are strongest when the Sun is especially active." },
  { id: 'cz003', diff: 3, topic: 'Mycorrhizal networks', text: "Beneath a forest floor lies a dense web of fungal threads that connect the roots of neighboring trees. In these partnerships, known as mycorrhizal networks, the fungi extract water and minerals from the soil and receive sugars from the trees in return. Some researchers suggest that the network also lets trees exchange nutrients and warning signals, although the extent of this cooperation remains debated. What is beyond doubt is that forests depend heavily on their hidden fungal partners." },
  { id: 'cz004', diff: 2, topic: 'Bioluminescence', text: "Bioluminescence is the ability of living organisms to produce their own light. It is especially common in the deep sea, where sunlight never reaches. Some fish use glowing lures to attract prey, while others flash lights to confuse predators or to find a mate. The light results from a chemical reaction inside special cells or from bacteria that live inside the animal. On land, fireflies are the best known example of this remarkable adaptation." },
  { id: 'cz005', diff: 4, topic: 'Superconductivity', text: "Superconductivity is the phenomenon whereby certain materials, when cooled below a critical temperature, conduct electric current with no resistance whatsoever. Once a current has been set up in a closed loop of such a material, it can circulate for years without any measurable decay. The practical difficulty is that most known superconductors demand extreme cooling, which makes them costly to operate. Consequently, the search for substances that retain this property at higher temperatures remains one of the most ambitious goals of physics." },
  { id: 'cz006', diff: 3, topic: 'Gene editing', text: "Gene editing tools based on a bacterial defense mechanism have transformed biological research over the past decade. The system uses a guide molecule to lead an enzyme to a precise location in the genome, where it cuts the DNA. The cell then repairs the break, and scientists can exploit this repair to disable a gene or insert a new sequence. Although the technique holds enormous therapeutic promise, it also raises serious ethical questions, particularly when changes could be passed on to future generations." },
  { id: 'cz007', diff: 3, topic: 'Gravitational waves', text: "Gravitational waves are ripples in the fabric of space and time that travel outward from violent cosmic events. Einstein predicted their existence a century ago, but they are so faint that detecting them seemed almost impossible. The first direct detection, made in the year two thousand fifteen, came from two black holes that spiraled together and merged. Since then, astronomers have gained a completely new way of observing the universe, one that does not rely on light at all." },
  { id: 'cz008', diff: 4, topic: 'Entropy', text: "Entropy is often loosely described as disorder, but it is more accurately understood as a measure of the number of microscopic arrangements compatible with a given overall state. The second law of thermodynamics asserts that, in an isolated system, entropy tends to increase, simply because disordered configurations vastly outnumber ordered ones. This statistical reasoning explains why a dropped cup shatters but never spontaneously reassembles. Living organisms appear to defy the law, yet they merely export disorder to their surroundings." },
  { id: 'cz009', diff: 2, topic: 'Lichens', text: "A lichen is not a single organism but a partnership between a fungus and an alga or bacterium. The fungus provides a protective structure and absorbs moisture, while its partner uses sunlight to produce food. Together they can survive on bare rock, tree bark, and even in polar deserts, where few other living things can grow. Because lichens absorb substances directly from the air, scientists often use them as sensitive indicators of air pollution." },
  { id: 'cz010', diff: 2, topic: 'Enzymes', text: "Enzymes are proteins that speed up chemical reactions in living cells. Without them, many processes, such as digesting food, would take far too long to keep an organism alive. Each enzyme has a specific shape that fits only certain molecules, much like a key fits a particular lock. Temperature and acidity can change this shape and reduce the enzyme's effectiveness, which is why a high fever can be dangerous. Enzymes are also widely used in industry, for example in making cheese." },
  { id: 'cz011', diff: 3, topic: 'Exoplanets', text: "Astronomers have now confirmed thousands of planets orbiting stars other than our Sun. Because these worlds are extremely dim compared with their host stars, most are found indirectly. In the transit method, a telescope records a tiny, regular dip in a star's brightness as a planet passes in front of it. The size of the dip reveals the planet's diameter, and the interval between dips reveals its orbit. This approach has shown that planets are the rule rather than the exception in our galaxy." },
  { id: 'cz012', diff: 2, topic: 'Earth magnetic field', text: "The Earth behaves like a giant magnet, surrounded by an invisible magnetic field. This field is generated deep inside the planet, where the movement of liquid iron in the outer core creates electric currents. It extends far into space and acts as a shield, deflecting most of the charged particles that stream from the Sun. Without this protection, the atmosphere would gradually be stripped away, and life on the surface would be exposed to dangerous radiation." },
  { id: 'cz013', diff: 2, topic: 'Fossils', text: "Fossils are the preserved remains or traces of ancient living things. They form only under special conditions, usually when a dead organism is buried quickly by mud or sand before it can decay. Over millions of years, minerals dissolved in groundwater slowly replace the original material, turning bones or shells into stone. Because the process is so unlikely, the fossil record is incomplete, and scientists must piece together the history of life from fragments." },
  { id: 'cz014', diff: 3, topic: 'Permafrost', text: "Permafrost is ground that has remained frozen for at least two consecutive years, and in some regions of the Arctic it has stayed frozen for thousands. Locked inside it are vast quantities of organic matter from long dead plants and animals. As global temperatures rise, the ground thaws, and microbes begin to decompose this material, releasing carbon dioxide and methane into the atmosphere. This creates a worrying feedback loop, since the additional warming may in turn accelerate further thawing." },
  { id: 'cz015', diff: 4, topic: 'Uncertainty principle', text: "The uncertainty principle, formulated by Werner Heisenberg, states that certain pairs of physical properties, such as the position and momentum of a particle, cannot both be known with unlimited precision at the same moment. This is frequently misread as a statement about clumsy measuring instruments. In fact, it reflects a fundamental feature of nature itself: particles do not possess perfectly defined values for both quantities simultaneously, and the more sharply one is pinned down, the more elusive the other becomes." },
  { id: 'cz016', diff: 2, topic: 'Plate tectonics', text: "The surface of the Earth is divided into enormous slabs of rock called tectonic plates. These plates float slowly on the hot, partly molten layer beneath them and move only a few centimeters each year. Where two plates collide, mountains rise or one plate sinks under the other. Where they pull apart, new crust forms. Most earthquakes and volcanoes occur along plate boundaries, which explains why certain regions are far more dangerous than others." },
  { id: 'cz017', diff: 3, topic: 'Convergent evolution', text: "Convergent evolution occurs when unrelated species independently develop similar features because they face similar environmental pressures. Dolphins and sharks, for instance, share a streamlined body and a dorsal fin, even though one is a mammal and the other a fish. Similarly, birds, bats, and insects all evolved wings through entirely separate pathways. Biologists value such cases because they suggest that natural selection often arrives at comparable solutions to the same physical challenges." },
  { id: 'cz018', diff: 4, topic: 'Dark matter', text: "Observations of galaxies reveal a conspicuous discrepancy: stars at the outer edges orbit so rapidly that the visible matter alone could not supply enough gravity to hold them in place. Astronomers therefore postulate an invisible substance, dubbed dark matter, which neither emits nor absorbs light yet appears to outweigh ordinary matter several times over. Despite decades of experiments, no candidate particle has been conclusively detected, and a minority of physicists argue that gravity itself may require modification." },
  // ---------- social sciences and history ----------
  { id: 'cz019', diff: 2, topic: 'Social psychology', text: "People often behave differently when they are part of a group than when they are alone. In a famous series of experiments, volunteers were asked to judge the length of lines, and many agreed with an obviously wrong answer given by the rest of the group. The researchers concluded that the desire to fit in can be stronger than confidence in one's own judgment. This tendency, called conformity, helps explain how mistaken beliefs can spread quickly." },
  { id: 'cz020', diff: 3, topic: 'Bystander effect', text: "The bystander effect describes the finding that individuals are less likely to help a person in need when other people are present. Psychologists attribute this partly to diffusion of responsibility: each witness assumes that someone else will take action. Ambiguity also plays a role, because people look to one another to judge whether a situation is truly an emergency. Training programs now teach the public to overcome this inertia by assigning specific tasks to specific individuals." },
  { id: 'cz021', diff: 2, topic: 'Demography', text: "Many wealthy countries are facing the challenge of an aging population. As people live longer and families have fewer children, the proportion of older citizens rises steadily. This puts pressure on pension systems and health services, because fewer workers must support a growing number of retirees. Governments have responded in different ways, for example by raising the retirement age, encouraging immigration, or offering incentives to families who wish to have more children." },
  { id: 'cz022', diff: 3, topic: 'Social capital', text: "Sociologists use the term social capital to describe the networks of relationships and the norms of trust that allow communities to function smoothly. Neighborhoods rich in such ties tend to enjoy lower crime, better health, and greater resilience after disasters. Yet social capital can have a darker side, since tight knit groups sometimes exclude outsiders or pressure members to conform. The challenge for policymakers is therefore to encourage connections that bridge differences rather than merely reinforce existing boundaries." },
  { id: 'cz023', diff: 4, topic: 'Cultural capital', text: "The sociologist Pierre Bourdieu argued that inequality is perpetuated not only through money but also through cultural resources, such as accent, taste, and familiarity with elite institutions. Children raised in privileged households absorb these dispositions almost imperceptibly, and schools, while professing neutrality, tend to reward precisely those habits. Success is thus misrecognized as individual talent rather than inherited advantage, which lends existing hierarchies an air of legitimacy that purely economic explanations would fail to capture." },
  { id: 'cz024', diff: 2, topic: 'Ancient Greece', text: "The city of Athens is often described as the birthplace of democracy. Around the fifth century before the common era, male citizens gathered in an assembly to debate laws and vote on important decisions. Officials were frequently chosen by lot rather than by election, so ordinary people had a real chance of holding power. However, women, slaves, and foreigners were excluded, which means that the system was far less inclusive than modern democracies." },
  { id: 'cz025', diff: 2, topic: 'Medieval history', text: "The Black Death reached Europe in the fourteenth century and killed a large share of the population within a few years. The disease spread along trade routes, carried by fleas that lived on rats. Cities were hit particularly hard because people lived close together and sanitation was poor. Paradoxically, the shortage of workers that followed gave the survivors more bargaining power, and wages rose, which gradually weakened the old system of serfdom." },
  { id: 'cz026', diff: 3, topic: 'Columbian exchange', text: "After the voyages of Columbus, plants, animals, and diseases began to cross the Atlantic on an unprecedented scale. Potatoes and maize transformed European and African diets, while horses and wheat altered life in the Americas. The exchange was far from benign, however. Smallpox and other illnesses, to which indigenous peoples had no immunity, devastated entire societies. Historians therefore regard this exchange as one of the most consequential and most tragic episodes in global history." },
  { id: 'cz027', diff: 3, topic: 'Printing and the Reformation', text: "The spread of printing in the sixteenth century enabled religious ideas to travel with remarkable speed. Pamphlets criticizing the church could be reproduced by the thousands and read aloud to those who could not read themselves. Reformers exploited the new medium skillfully, translating the Bible into local languages so that ordinary believers could engage with it directly. Church authorities struggled to suppress these publications, which illustrates how a new technology can undermine established institutions." },
  { id: 'cz028', diff: 4, topic: 'Historical contingency', text: "Historians have long wrestled with the question of whether outcomes were inevitable or whether they hinged on chance events. Proponents of contingency maintain that a different decision, a delayed message, or an unexpected death could have sent events down an entirely separate path. Their opponents counter that deep structural forces, such as demography and economics, constrain what individuals can accomplish. Most scholars now seek a middle course, acknowledging that structures establish the range of possibilities within which contingent episodes operate." },
  { id: 'cz029', diff: 2, topic: 'Women suffrage', text: "The campaign for women's right to vote lasted for decades in many countries. Activists organized petitions, peaceful marches, and public speeches, and some were arrested for their protests. New Zealand became the first self governing country to grant women the vote in the late nineteenth century, and many others followed after the First World War. The movement demonstrated that lasting political change usually requires patience, organization, and the support of a broad coalition." },
  { id: 'cz030', diff: 3, topic: 'Archaeology', text: "Archaeologists reconstruct past societies from the material traces that people left behind, such as pottery, tools, and the foundations of buildings. Because most evidence has been damaged or lost, the discipline relies heavily on inference. Radiocarbon dating, for example, measures the decay of a radioactive form of carbon in organic remains to estimate their age. Yet even precise dates tell us little about beliefs or social relationships, which researchers can only guess at by comparing different sites." },
  { id: 'cz031', diff: 2, topic: 'Industrial labor', text: "During the nineteenth century, millions of people left the countryside to work in factories. Conditions were often harsh: shifts lasted many hours, wages were low, and machines were dangerous. Children frequently worked alongside adults. Over time, workers formed unions and demanded shorter days, safer workplaces, and fair pay. Reformers and governments eventually introduced laws that limited working hours and banned child labor, laying the foundations of modern employment protection." },
  { id: 'cz032', diff: 4, topic: 'Nationalism', text: "Nationalism is frequently presented as an ancient and natural attachment, yet many scholars contend that nations are modern constructions. They point out that standardized languages, national museums, and shared schooling were deliberately cultivated by states seeking the loyalty of diverse populations. The resulting communities are imagined, in the sense that most members will never meet one another, but they are no less powerful for that. Indeed, their emotional force explains both their capacity to unite and their capacity to destroy." },
  { id: 'cz033', diff: 3, topic: 'Game theory', text: "Game theory studies how rational individuals make decisions when the outcome depends on the choices of others. In the well known prisoner's dilemma, two suspects would both fare better by staying silent, yet each has an incentive to betray the other. The result is a collectively worse outcome that nobody intended. The same logic appears in disputes between rival firms and between neighboring countries, which is why economists and diplomats pay close attention to it." },
  { id: 'cz034', diff: 2, topic: 'Migration', text: "People migrate for many reasons, including the search for work, safety, or education. Researchers often distinguish between push factors, which drive people away from their homes, and pull factors, which attract them to a new place. War, poverty, and natural disasters are typical push factors, while higher wages and family connections pull migrants toward certain destinations. Migration can benefit both sides, since host countries gain workers and sending countries receive money transfers from relatives abroad." },
  { id: 'cz035', diff: 4, topic: 'Survivorship bias', text: "During the Second World War, analysts examined returning bombers to decide where additional armor should be placed. The obvious suggestion was to reinforce the areas riddled with bullet holes. The statistician Abraham Wald perceived the flaw: the aircraft under study were precisely those that had survived, so the undamaged areas were the ones that proved fatal when hit. This episode is now a classic warning that conclusions drawn from visible successes may be systematically distorted by what remains unseen." },
  { id: 'cz036', diff: 3, topic: 'Anthropology of gifts', text: "Anthropologists have long been fascinated by the exchange of gifts, which appears to be voluntary but often carries a strong obligation to reciprocate. In many societies, the giving of presents establishes alliances, expresses status, and creates a web of mutual debt that holds communities together. Marcel Mauss argued that such exchanges are never purely generous, since refusing or failing to return a gift can damage relationships. His insights remain influential in studies of modern economies as well." },
  // ---------- technology ----------
  { id: 'cz037', diff: 2, topic: 'Cloud computing', text: "Cloud computing allows people and companies to use computing power and storage over the internet instead of owning their own equipment. A business can rent as much capacity as it needs and pay only for what it uses. This is especially useful for new companies, which avoid large initial costs. However, relying on a distant provider also creates risks, such as service interruptions and concerns about who can access sensitive data." },
  { id: 'cz038', diff: 3, topic: 'Algorithmic bias', text: "Algorithms trained on historical data may reproduce the prejudices embedded in that data. A recruitment tool, for instance, learned to favor male applicants because past hiring decisions had favored men. Such outcomes are troubling precisely because they appear objective, which discourages scrutiny. Researchers therefore advocate regular audits, more diverse training data, and transparent documentation, while acknowledging that no technical fix can fully replace human responsibility for the decisions that these systems influence." },
  { id: 'cz039', diff: 2, topic: 'Batteries', text: "Rechargeable lithium batteries power almost every portable device, from phones to electric cars. During charging, lithium ions move from one electrode to another, and they travel back when the device is in use. Over time, the electrodes degrade, so the battery holds less energy. Engineers are working on materials that store more power, charge faster, and last longer, because better batteries are essential for storing electricity from wind and solar farms." },
  { id: 'cz040', diff: 3, topic: 'Quantum computing', text: "Conventional computers process information in bits, each of which is either zero or one. Quantum computers use quantum bits, which can exist in a combination of both states at once. For certain problems, such as simulating molecules, this property could allow calculations that would take ordinary machines an impractical amount of time. Nevertheless, quantum systems are extremely fragile, and even slight disturbances introduce errors, so building reliable large machines remains a formidable engineering challenge." },
  { id: 'cz041', diff: 2, topic: 'Satellites', text: "Thousands of satellites orbit the Earth, and many of them affect our daily lives. Some transmit television and telephone signals, while others photograph the weather or monitor crops and forests from above. Navigation systems depend on a network of satellites that send precise timing signals, allowing a small receiver to calculate its location within a few meters. As more satellites are launched, however, the risk of collisions and space debris increases." },
  { id: 'cz042', diff: 4, topic: 'Blockchain consensus', text: "A distributed ledger dispenses with a central authority by requiring thousands of independent participants to agree on the state of the record. Achieving such consensus among parties that may not trust one another is a notoriously difficult problem, and different systems resolve it through different mechanisms, each with distinctive trade offs between security, speed, and energy consumption. The elegance of the arrangement should not obscure its limitations, since an immutable record is only as reliable as the information originally entered into it." },
  { id: 'cz043', diff: 3, topic: 'Printing in three dimensions', text: "Additive manufacturing, commonly known as three dimensional printing, builds objects layer by layer from a digital design. Unlike traditional methods that cut away material, it uses only what is needed, which reduces waste. The technique is already used to produce customized medical implants, lightweight aircraft components, and prototypes. Its limitations include slow production speed and the relatively high cost of materials, which currently prevent it from replacing mass production for most everyday goods." },
  { id: 'cz044', diff: 2, topic: 'Privacy online', text: "Every time we use a website or an application, we leave behind traces of data. Companies collect this information to improve their services and to show advertisements that match our interests. Many users, however, do not realize how much is being gathered. Experts recommend reading privacy settings carefully, using strong and different passwords, and limiting the personal details shared online. In many regions, new laws also give people the right to see and delete the data held about them." },
  { id: 'cz045', diff: 3, topic: 'Automation and work', text: "Economists disagree about how automation will affect employment. Some argue that machines will replace workers in routine tasks, leaving many people without a livelihood. Others point out that technological change has always eliminated certain jobs while creating new ones that were previously unimaginable. What seems clear is that the transition is rarely painless: workers whose skills become obsolete may not easily move into the new roles, which highlights the importance of retraining and education." },
  // ---------- health ----------
  { id: 'cz046', diff: 2, topic: 'Sleep and memory', text: "Sleep is not simply a period of rest for the brain. During deep sleep, the brain replays the events of the day and strengthens important memories while discarding less useful details. Students who sleep well after studying tend to remember more than those who stay awake all night. Lack of sleep also makes it harder to concentrate and to control emotions, which is why doctors recommend that teenagers and adults protect their sleeping hours." },
  { id: 'cz047', diff: 3, topic: 'Antibiotic resistance', text: "Bacteria reproduce rapidly, and occasional mutations allow some of them to survive exposure to an antibiotic. These survivors multiply, so that over time the drug loses its effectiveness. Overuse and misuse of antibiotics, for example in treating viral infections or in livestock farming, accelerate the process. Public health experts warn that without new drugs and more responsible prescribing, routine operations and common infections could once again become life threatening." },
  { id: 'cz048', diff: 2, topic: 'Stress', text: "A small amount of stress can be helpful, because it prepares the body to react quickly to danger. The heart beats faster, and hormones release energy into the bloodstream. Problems arise when stress continues for weeks or months. Long term stress is linked to headaches, sleep problems, and a weaker immune system. Regular exercise, social support, and relaxation techniques such as slow breathing can help people to manage pressure more effectively." },
  { id: 'cz049', diff: 3, topic: 'Epidemiology', text: "Epidemiologists investigate how diseases spread through populations and which factors increase the risk of illness. Their work often begins with a careful comparison between those who fell ill and those who did not. A classic example occurred in nineteenth century London, when the physician John Snow mapped cholera cases and traced them to a single contaminated water pump. His methodical reasoning showed that the disease was transmitted through water, long before the existence of bacteria was widely accepted." },
  { id: 'cz050', diff: 4, topic: 'Placebo ethics', text: "Clinical trials customarily compare a new treatment with an inert substance, so that genuine pharmacological effects can be distinguished from the patient's expectations. Yet withholding an effective therapy from the control group is ethically fraught whenever one already exists. Regulators have accordingly tightened the conditions under which placebos may be used, insisting that participants be fully informed and that no one be exposed to serious harm. The tension between scientific rigor and the duty of care remains unresolved." },
  { id: 'cz051', diff: 2, topic: 'Mental health', text: "Mental health problems are far more common than many people think. Conditions such as depression and anxiety affect millions of people at some point in their lives. Unfortunately, those who suffer often hesitate to ask for help because they fear being judged. Education campaigns try to change these attitudes by explaining that mental illnesses are real medical conditions. With early support and appropriate treatment, most people improve significantly." },
  { id: 'cz052', diff: 3, topic: 'Nudges in health', text: "Public health authorities increasingly rely on small changes in the environment to guide behavior without restricting choice. Placing fruit at eye level in a cafeteria, for example, raises its consumption without forbidding anything else. Because such measures preserve freedom, they are often described as nudges. Critics, however, question whether subtle steering is truly respectful of autonomy, particularly when people are unaware that their decisions are being shaped by the way options are arranged." },
  { id: 'cz053', diff: 2, topic: 'Vaccination programs', text: "When a large majority of a community is vaccinated, a disease finds it difficult to spread. This indirect protection, known as herd immunity, also shelters people who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborn babies or patients with weak immune systems. Smallpox was eliminated worldwide through a determined vaccination campaign. However, if the proportion of vaccinated people drops, outbreaks of diseases such as measles can quickly return." },
  { id: 'cz054', diff: 3, topic: 'Chronic disease', text: "In wealthy countries, chronic illnesses such as heart disease and diabetes have replaced infectious diseases as the leading causes of death. These conditions develop slowly and are closely tied to lifestyle factors, including diet, physical inactivity, and smoking. Treating them is expensive, since patients may require medication and monitoring for decades. Consequently, many health systems are shifting their emphasis from curing illness toward preventing it through education and early screening." },
  // ---------- environment ----------
  { id: 'cz055', diff: 2, topic: 'Ocean acidification', text: "The oceans absorb roughly a quarter of the carbon dioxide that humans release into the air. When this gas dissolves in seawater, it forms a weak acid, and the water gradually becomes more acidic. This change makes it harder for shellfish and corals to build their hard skeletons. Because these creatures are the foundation of many marine food chains, scientists worry that rising acidity could disrupt entire ocean ecosystems." },
  { id: 'cz056', diff: 3, topic: 'Biodiversity loss', text: "Species are disappearing at a rate far above the natural background level, and human activity is the principal cause. Habitat destruction, overexploitation, pollution, and invasive species all play a part, while climate change increasingly compounds these pressures. The loss matters not only for moral reasons but also because ecosystems provide services such as pollination and water purification on which societies depend. Conservationists therefore argue that protecting nature is an investment rather than a luxury." },
  { id: 'cz057', diff: 2, topic: 'Wetlands', text: "Wetlands, such as marshes and swamps, are areas where water covers the soil for much of the year. They act like natural sponges, absorbing heavy rainfall and reducing the risk of floods. They also filter pollutants from water and provide a home for many birds, fish, and insects. Despite these benefits, large areas of wetland have been drained for farming and construction, and conservation groups are now working to restore them." },
  { id: 'cz058', diff: 3, topic: 'Circular economy', text: "The traditional economic model follows a linear path: raw materials are extracted, turned into products, and eventually thrown away. A circular economy seeks to break this pattern by designing goods that can be repaired, reused, and recycled. Manufacturers might lease products rather than sell them, retaining responsibility for the materials. Advocates claim that this approach reduces both waste and dependence on scarce resources, although it requires coordination across entire supply chains." },
  { id: 'cz059', diff: 4, topic: 'Carbon pricing', text: "Economists generally regard putting a price on carbon emissions as the most efficient way to reduce them, since it lets each polluter decide where cutting is cheapest. Whether implemented through a tax or a tradable permit scheme, the mechanism internalizes a cost that would otherwise fall on society at large. Political resistance nonetheless remains considerable, partly because the burden is visible and immediate whereas the benefits are diffuse and delayed, and partly because poorer households spend a larger share of income on energy." },
  { id: 'cz060', diff: 2, topic: 'Deforestation', text: "Forests are being cleared at a rapid pace to make room for farms, roads, and cities. Trees absorb carbon dioxide from the air, so cutting them down contributes to climate change. Deforestation also destroys the habitat of countless animals and can lead to soil erosion, because tree roots no longer hold the earth in place. Many governments now support replanting programs and sustainable forestry to protect the forests that remain." },
  { id: 'cz061', diff: 3, topic: 'Rewilding', text: "Rewilding is a conservation approach that aims to restore natural processes by reintroducing species that once lived in an area. When wolves returned to Yellowstone National Park, deer changed their grazing habits, which allowed trees to regrow along riverbanks. The effects rippled through the ecosystem, benefiting beavers, birds, and fish. Critics caution that such projects can conflict with farmers and local residents, so success depends on sustained dialogue with the communities involved." },
  { id: 'cz062', diff: 2, topic: 'Renewable water', text: "Fresh water is much scarcer than most people realize. Although the planet is covered mostly by oceans, only a tiny fraction of its water is fresh, and much of that is locked in ice. Population growth, farming, and industry are putting increasing pressure on rivers and underground reserves. Solutions include fixing leaking pipes, recycling wastewater, and growing crops that need less irrigation, all of which help communities to use this precious resource wisely." },
  { id: 'cz063', diff: 3, topic: 'Urban heat islands', text: "Cities are often several degrees warmer than the surrounding countryside, a phenomenon known as the urban heat island effect. Dark pavements and rooftops absorb sunlight, while concrete buildings release the stored heat slowly at night. The lack of vegetation reduces natural cooling through evaporation. During heat waves, this extra warmth can be deadly for elderly residents. Planners are responding with green roofs, reflective materials, and additional trees, which together can lower local temperatures noticeably." },
  // ---------- linguistics ----------
  { id: 'cz064', diff: 2, topic: 'Language change', text: "All living languages change constantly. New words appear to describe new inventions, while others fall out of use. Pronunciation shifts gradually from one generation to the next, and grammar rules slowly evolve. Some people regard these changes as a sign of decline, but linguists see them as natural and unavoidable. English today differs so greatly from the language of Shakespeare that modern readers often need notes to understand his plays." },
  { id: 'cz065', diff: 3, topic: 'Language acquisition', text: "Children acquire their first language with astonishing speed and without formal instruction. By the age of five, most have mastered the complex grammar of their mother tongue. Some theorists argue that humans are born with an innate capacity for language, while others emphasize the role of interaction and exposure. Evidence from children who are isolated from speech suggests that there may be a sensitive period, after which learning a language becomes considerably harder." },
  { id: 'cz066', diff: 4, topic: 'Pragmatics', text: "Much of what speakers communicate is not contained in the literal meaning of their words. If a colleague, asked whether a presentation went well, replies that the room was very well heated, the listener readily infers a negative judgment. Pragmatics investigates how such implications are generated and understood, relying on shared assumptions about cooperation and relevance. Misunderstandings across cultures often arise because these unspoken conventions differ, even when the vocabulary and grammar are perfectly mastered." },
  { id: 'cz067', diff: 2, topic: 'Sign languages', text: "Sign languages are complete languages with their own grammar and vocabulary, not simple gestures that imitate spoken words. They use hand shapes, movements, facial expressions, and the space around the signer to express meaning. Different countries have different sign languages, and they are not mutually understandable. Deaf children who are exposed to sign language early develop language skills in the same way and at the same pace as hearing children." },
  { id: 'cz068', diff: 3, topic: 'Etymology', text: "The history of a word often reveals the history of the people who used it. English contains a large number of words of French origin because the Normans conquered England in the eleventh century, and their language became associated with law and government. As a result, the language of the kitchen sometimes differs from the language of the farm: we raise cattle but eat beef, an echo of the social divide between the English speaking peasants and their French speaking rulers." },
  { id: 'cz069', diff: 2, topic: 'Accents', text: "Everyone speaks with an accent, although we tend to notice only those that differ from our own. An accent reflects where a person grew up and the social groups they belong to. Research shows that listeners sometimes make unfair judgments about intelligence or education based on accents alone. Employers and teachers are increasingly encouraged to recognize this prejudice, since clear communication does not depend on sounding like any particular group." },
  { id: 'cz070', diff: 4, topic: 'Translation', text: "Translation is never a matter of replacing words one by one, since languages carve up experience in different ways. A term loaded with cultural connotation may have no counterpart, forcing the translator to choose between fidelity to the original wording and fluency for the new reader. Poetry poses the sharpest dilemma, because sound and rhythm contribute as much as meaning. Accomplished translators therefore regard their task as a form of interpretation, and they accept that some loss is unavoidable." },
  // ---------- arts ----------
  { id: 'cz071', diff: 2, topic: 'Photography', text: "Photography was invented in the nineteenth century and quickly changed the way people saw the world. For the first time, ordinary families could afford a realistic portrait. Painters, who had long been valued for their ability to copy reality, began to explore other goals, such as capturing mood and light. Today, digital cameras and smartphones allow billions of images to be taken every day, and photography has become a form of both art and everyday communication." },
  { id: 'cz072', diff: 3, topic: 'Cinema history', text: "The earliest films were silent, and audiences usually watched them accompanied by live music. The arrival of synchronized sound at the end of the nineteen twenties transformed the industry almost overnight. Many established stars lost their careers because their voices did not suit the new medium, while theaters invested heavily in new equipment. The shift illustrates how technological innovation can reshape an art form, rewarding some practitioners and leaving others behind." },
  { id: 'cz073', diff: 2, topic: 'Architecture and light', text: "Architects pay close attention to the way natural light enters a building. Large windows can brighten a room and reduce the need for electric lamps, which saves energy. On the other hand, too much direct sunlight can make a space uncomfortably hot. Designers therefore use features such as overhanging roofs, shutters, and special glass. In museums and churches, light is also used to create a particular atmosphere and to guide the eye of visitors." },
  { id: 'cz074', diff: 3, topic: 'Literary realism', text: "In the nineteenth century, a group of novelists rejected the idealized heroes and dramatic plots of romanticism. Instead, they depicted ordinary people in everyday settings, paying meticulous attention to social conditions and the pressures of money. This movement, known as realism, treated the novel as a means of understanding society. Its characters are rarely wholly good or wholly bad, and their fates frequently depend on circumstances that lie beyond their control." },
  { id: 'cz075', diff: 4, topic: 'Aesthetics', text: "Philosophers of art have long disputed whether beauty resides in objects or in the eye of the observer. Defenders of objectivity point to the remarkable convergence of judgment about certain masterpieces across centuries and cultures. Skeptics reply that apparent agreement masks the influence of education, fashion, and power, which determine what is canonized. A subtler position holds that aesthetic judgments, though grounded in individual experience, nevertheless claim universal assent, and this claim is precisely what distinguishes them from mere preferences." },
  { id: 'cz076', diff: 2, topic: 'Music and emotion', text: "Music has a powerful effect on human emotions. A slow melody in a minor key often sounds sad, while a fast rhythm can make us feel energetic. Scientists have found that listening to music we enjoy releases chemicals in the brain that are linked to pleasure. Hospitals sometimes use music to reduce anxiety before operations, and many people play favorite songs to stay motivated while they exercise or study." },
  { id: 'cz077', diff: 3, topic: 'Museums and restitution', text: "Many major museums hold artifacts that were removed from their countries of origin during the colonial era. Source nations increasingly demand their return, arguing that these objects are central to their cultural identity. Museums often respond that they offer safe storage and access to a global public. Recent years have nonetheless seen a number of notable returns, suggesting that institutions are beginning to reconsider the ethical foundations of their collections." },
  // ---------- urban planning ----------
  { id: 'cz078', diff: 2, topic: 'Walkable cities', text: "A walkable city is one where residents can reach shops, schools, and workplaces on foot. Such neighborhoods have wide sidewalks, safe crossings, and a mixture of homes and businesses close together. Walking reduces traffic and pollution, and it also improves health. Studies show that people living in walkable areas tend to exercise more and to know more of their neighbors, which strengthens the sense of community." },
  { id: 'cz079', diff: 3, topic: 'Zoning', text: "Zoning laws divide cities into areas where only certain kinds of building are permitted, such as residential, commercial, or industrial districts. Originally intended to separate homes from polluting factories, zoning has since had unintended consequences. Strict limits on density can raise housing prices, and the separation of functions forces residents to depend on cars. Many planners now favor more flexible rules that allow apartments, shops, and offices to coexist within the same neighborhood." },
  { id: 'cz080', diff: 2, topic: 'Public squares', text: "Public squares have been the heart of cities since ancient times. They provide open spaces where people can meet, trade, celebrate, or protest. A successful square usually has seating, shade, and nearby cafes that keep it lively throughout the day. When a square is poorly designed or feels unsafe, people avoid it, and the space becomes empty. Good planning therefore considers not only the size of the area but also how people will actually use it." },
  { id: 'cz081', diff: 3, topic: 'Informal settlements', text: "In many fast growing cities, a large part of the population lives in informal settlements, built without legal permission on land that residents do not own. These neighborhoods frequently lack clean water, sanitation, and secure tenure. Demolition rarely solves the problem, since displaced families simply settle elsewhere. Increasingly, policymakers favor upgrading these areas by providing basic services and granting legal recognition, which encourages residents to invest in improving their own homes." },
  { id: 'cz082', diff: 4, topic: 'Induced demand', text: "Transport planners have repeatedly observed that widening a congested highway offers only fleeting relief. Faster travel makes driving more attractive, so additional motorists soon fill the new capacity, a phenomenon known as induced demand. The implication is counterintuitive: reducing congestion may depend less on supplying more road space than on managing demand through pricing, improved public transport, or denser development that shortens trips. Resistance persists, however, because the immediate benefits of construction are tangible while its long term futility is not." },
  { id: 'cz083', diff: 2, topic: 'Green roofs', text: "A green roof is a roof covered with soil and plants. It keeps buildings cooler in summer and warmer in winter, so owners spend less on energy. The plants also absorb rainwater, which reduces the pressure on drains during storms, and they provide a habitat for insects and birds. Although installing a green roof costs more than building an ordinary one, the long term savings and environmental benefits make it attractive to many cities." },
  { id: 'cz084', diff: 3, topic: 'Bike infrastructure', text: "Cities that invest in protected cycle lanes see a rise in the number of people who cycle, including those who would never ride in heavy traffic. Separating bicycles from cars matters more than any promotional campaign, because the main obstacle to cycling is fear of collisions. Copenhagen and Amsterdam offer well known examples, though their success rests on decades of consistent policy and a culture that treats cycling as ordinary transport." },
  { id: 'cz085', diff: 2, topic: 'Housing supply', text: "When a city attracts new residents faster than it builds homes, prices and rents rise. Some governments respond by limiting rents, which helps current tenants but may discourage owners from maintaining or offering properties. Others simplify building rules or release public land for construction. Most economists argue that increasing supply is the most reliable long term solution, although the process can be slow and often meets opposition from existing neighbors." },
  { id: 'cz086', diff: 3, topic: 'Transit oriented development', text: "Transit oriented development concentrates housing, jobs, and services around railway or metro stations. Residents can reach many destinations without a car, which lowers emissions and household transport costs. The model also helps public transport operators, since dense surroundings guarantee a steady flow of passengers. A persistent difficulty is that property values near stations tend to climb, so planners must take care that existing residents are not priced out of the very neighborhoods that benefit." },
  { id: 'cz087', diff: 4, topic: 'Jane Jacobs', text: "In the middle of the twentieth century, Jane Jacobs challenged the prevailing orthodoxy of urban renewal, which favored demolishing crowded neighborhoods in favor of towers and highways. She argued that lively streets depend on mixed uses, short blocks, old buildings, and the informal watchfulness of residents, which she famously called eyes on the street. Her observations, drawn from daily experience rather than abstract theory, reshaped planning thinking and continue to inform debates about what makes a city livable." },
  { id: 'cz088', diff: 2, topic: 'Noise in cities', text: "Cities are noisy places, and constant sound can harm the people who live there. Traffic, construction, and nightlife make it harder to sleep and can raise stress levels. Over many years, exposure to loud noise has even been linked to heart problems. Planners reduce the problem by building barriers beside highways, using quieter road surfaces, and creating calm zones such as parks where residents can escape the din." },
  { id: 'cz089', diff: 3, topic: 'Water management in cities', text: "Traditional drainage systems rush rainwater away from cities as quickly as possible, which often overloads rivers and causes flooding downstream. A more recent philosophy, sometimes called the sponge city, aims instead to absorb and store rain where it falls. Permeable pavements, rain gardens, and small ponds allow water to soak into the ground slowly. These solutions are cheaper than enlarging pipes and bring additional benefits, such as cooler air and greener neighborhoods." },
  { id: 'cz090', diff: 2, topic: 'Libraries', text: "Public libraries have changed greatly in recent decades. Although people borrow fewer printed books than before, libraries remain very popular. Many now offer free internet access, language classes, and quiet places to work. For people who cannot afford a computer or a home office, they provide essential services. Librarians also help visitors to find reliable information, which is an increasingly valuable skill in a world full of online claims." },
  // ---------- finance, economics, accounting, management, business ethics ----------
  { id: 'cz091', diff: 2, topic: 'Interest rates', text: "An interest rate is the price of borrowing money. When a bank lends funds to a customer, it charges interest as payment for the risk and for the time during which the money cannot be used elsewhere. Savers receive interest too, although at a lower rate, and the difference is how banks earn a profit. Central banks influence these rates for the whole economy. When rates are low, borrowing becomes cheaper, and households and firms tend to spend and invest more." },
  { id: 'cz092', diff: 3, topic: 'Compound interest', text: "Compound interest means that interest is calculated not only on the original sum but also on the interest already accumulated. Over short periods, the difference from simple interest is barely noticeable. Over decades, however, the effect becomes dramatic, because growth builds upon itself. This is why financial advisers urge young people to begin saving early, even in modest amounts. The same arithmetic works against borrowers, whose unpaid credit card balances can expand at an alarming pace." },
  { id: 'cz093', diff: 4, topic: 'Time value of money', text: "A cornerstone of finance is the proposition that a sum received today is worth more than the same sum promised in the future. The reasons are threefold: money in hand can be invested to earn a return, inflation erodes purchasing power, and any future payment carries some risk of default. Analysts therefore discount expected cash flows at a rate reflecting these considerations, and the present value obtained allows projects of very different durations to be compared on a common footing." },
  { id: 'cz094', diff: 2, topic: 'Balance sheet', text: "A balance sheet is a financial statement that shows what a company owns and what it owes at a particular date. The things it owns, such as cash, equipment, and buildings, are called assets. The amounts owed to banks and suppliers are called liabilities. The difference between the two is the owners' share, known as equity. The statement is called a balance sheet because assets must always equal liabilities plus equity." },
  { id: 'cz095', diff: 3, topic: 'Cash flow', text: "A company can report a healthy profit and still run out of money. This apparent paradox arises because profit is an accounting measure that records sales when they are made, not when customers actually pay. If clients take months to settle their invoices while suppliers and employees must be paid immediately, the firm may be unable to meet its obligations. For this reason, managers monitor cash flow closely, and many growing businesses fail through a shortage of liquidity rather than a lack of customers." },
  { id: 'cz096', diff: 4, topic: 'Accrual accounting', text: "Under accrual accounting, revenues are recognized when they are earned and expenses when they are incurred, irrespective of when cash changes hands. This convention gives a more faithful picture of performance over a period than a simple record of receipts and payments could provide. It also, however, demands numerous judgments about timing and valuation, such as when a long contract has been sufficiently completed, and those judgments create opportunities for both honest error and deliberate distortion." },
  { id: 'cz097', diff: 3, topic: 'Opportunity cost', text: "Economists define the cost of any choice as the value of the best alternative that is given up. A student who spends an afternoon at a concert has not merely paid for a ticket; she has also forgone the wages she could have earned or the revision she could have done. Recognizing opportunity cost encourages more careful decisions, because it reminds us that resources, whether time or money, are limited and that every use of them excludes other possibilities." },
  { id: 'cz098', diff: 2, topic: 'Budget deficits', text: "A government runs a budget deficit when it spends more than it collects in taxes during a year. To cover the gap, it borrows money by selling bonds to investors. Small deficits can be useful during a recession, because extra spending supports jobs and demand. However, years of large deficits add to the national debt, and the interest payments on that debt can eventually take a large share of the budget." },
  { id: 'cz099', diff: 4, topic: 'Liquidity trap', text: "Ordinarily, a central bank stimulates a sluggish economy by lowering interest rates. When rates approach zero, however, this instrument loses its potency: households and banks, fearful of the future, hoard cash rather than lend or spend it, regardless of how cheap borrowing becomes. This predicament, termed a liquidity trap, helps explain why monetary authorities in several countries resorted to unconventional measures, such as purchasing bonds on a massive scale, in the aftermath of the financial crisis." },
  { id: 'cz100', diff: 3, topic: 'Comparative advantage in practice', text: "Even a lawyer who types faster than her assistant may do well to hire him. Her time is more valuable when spent on legal work, so every hour devoted to typing carries a high opportunity cost. The same logic applies to nations: each should specialize in what it produces at the lowest relative cost and trade for the rest. Though simple, this idea underpins the case for open markets, even if its benefits are rarely shared evenly." },
  { id: 'cz101', diff: 2, topic: 'Entrepreneurial financing', text: "New companies need money to grow, and they have several ways to find it. Founders may use their own savings or borrow from family and friends. Banks are often cautious about lending to businesses without a track record. As an alternative, venture capital firms invest in promising start ups in exchange for a share of the ownership. This kind of funding provides large sums, but founders must accept that they will give up some control." },
  { id: 'cz102', diff: 3, topic: 'Risk and return', text: "A basic principle of investing is that higher expected returns come with higher risk. Government bonds from stable countries offer modest but reliable income, whereas shares in young companies may yield spectacular gains or disappear entirely. Investors differ in their tolerance for uncertainty, depending on age, wealth, and temperament. Sensible portfolios therefore reflect not the pursuit of maximum return, but a deliberate balance between the growth an investor desires and the losses he can afford." },
  { id: 'cz103', diff: 4, topic: 'Market bubbles', text: "Asset bubbles form when prices rise far above any reasonable estimate of underlying value, sustained chiefly by the expectation that others will pay still more. Participants often recognize the danger yet continue buying, convinced that they can leave before the collapse. The Dutch tulip mania and the dotcom boom are frequently cited, although economists disagree about whether bubbles can be identified in real time. What is undisputed is that their bursting inflicts damage reaching well beyond the speculators involved." },
  { id: 'cz104', diff: 2, topic: 'Inflation and savings', text: "When prices rise steadily, each unit of money buys fewer goods than before. This means that cash kept at home slowly loses its value. If a savings account pays two percent interest but prices rise by three percent, the real value of the savings actually decreases. For this reason, people who want to protect their wealth often look for investments that grow faster than inflation, such as shares or property." },
  { id: 'cz105', diff: 3, topic: 'Corporate social responsibility', text: "Some economists maintain that the sole responsibility of a company is to maximize profit for its owners within the law. Others contend that firms also owe obligations to employees, communities, and the environment, since they depend on these groups for their long term success. Many corporations now publish sustainability reports and adopt ethical codes. Skeptics warn that such efforts may amount to little more than public relations unless they are backed by measurable commitments and independent verification." },
  { id: 'cz106', diff: 4, topic: 'Agency problem', text: "In large corporations, ownership and control are separated: shareholders supply the capital, but professional managers take the decisions. Because the interests of the two groups need not coincide, managers may pursue personal goals, such as prestige or generous perks, at the expense of those who employ them. Boards, audits, and incentive contracts are devised to align these interests, yet each solution brings its own distortions, as when stock based bonuses encourage executives to inflate short term results." },
  { id: 'cz107', diff: 2, topic: 'Leadership', text: "Effective managers do more than give orders. They set clear goals, listen to their staff, and explain the reasons behind their decisions. Research suggests that employees work harder and stay longer in organizations where they feel respected and trusted. Good leaders also admit their own mistakes, which encourages others to be honest about problems. In this way, a positive working culture helps a company to solve difficulties before they become serious." },
  { id: 'cz108', diff: 3, topic: 'Conflicts of interest', text: "A conflict of interest arises when a professional's personal interests could compromise the judgment he is expected to exercise on behalf of others. A financial adviser who earns higher commissions on certain products, for instance, may be tempted to recommend them regardless of the client's needs. Ethical codes therefore require disclosure, and in serious cases abstention from the decision altogether. The mere appearance of a conflict can damage trust, even when no wrongdoing has actually occurred." },
  { id: 'cz109', diff: 3, topic: 'Fair value accounting', text: "Traditional accounting records assets at their original purchase price, which is objective but may become outdated. Fair value accounting instead reports them at what they could currently be sold for. Supporters argue that this gives investors timely and relevant information. Critics respond that during a panic, market prices can collapse irrationally, forcing institutions to record losses that may never materialize. The debate illustrates the permanent tension in accounting between relevance and reliability." },
  { id: 'cz110', diff: 4, topic: 'Goodhart law', text: "When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure. This observation, attributed to the economist Charles Goodhart, captures a pervasive hazard in management. Once employees know that they will be evaluated on a single indicator, they adjust their behavior to improve that number, sometimes at the expense of the very objective it was meant to represent. Hospitals that are judged on waiting times, for example, may reclassify patients rather than treat them faster." },
  { id: 'cz111', diff: 2, topic: 'Consumer behavior', text: "Shoppers do not always make rational choices. Stores often place popular items at the back so that customers walk past many other products. Prices ending in ninety nine make a product seem cheaper than it really is, and signs announcing a limited offer create a feeling of urgency. Understanding these techniques helps consumers resist impulse purchases, and many people now make a list before shopping to stay focused on what they really need." },
  { id: 'cz112', diff: 3, topic: 'Loss aversion', text: "Experiments by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky revealed that people feel the pain of a loss roughly twice as strongly as the pleasure of an equivalent gain. This asymmetry, called loss aversion, explains why investors tend to hold on to losing shares in the hope of recovering their money, while selling winners too early. It also helps account for the reluctance to change jobs or suppliers, even when the alternative looks objectively better." },
  { id: 'cz113', diff: 4, topic: 'Information asymmetry', text: "George Akerlof demonstrated that markets can unravel when one party knows far more than the other. Sellers of used cars, for instance, are aware of defects that buyers cannot observe, so buyers offer only an average price, which drives owners of good cars out of the market. What remains is a pool of ever poorer quality. Warranties, inspections, and reputation serve as mechanisms through which trustworthy sellers may signal their quality and thereby rescue otherwise failing exchanges." },
  { id: 'cz114', diff: 3, topic: 'Central bank balance sheets', text: "After the financial crisis, several central banks bought huge quantities of government bonds and other securities, a policy known as quantitative easing. By purchasing these assets, they pushed up prices, lowered long term interest rates, and injected money into the banking system. Supporters credit the policy with averting a deeper recession. Detractors fear that it inflates asset prices, benefits the wealthy, and will prove difficult to reverse without disturbing financial markets." },
  { id: 'cz115', diff: 4, topic: 'Regulatory capture', text: "Regulators are supposed to serve the public, yet they often depend on the very industries they oversee for expertise and, eventually, for employment. Over time, this closeness may lead them to adopt the perspective of the firms they supervise, a phenomenon economists label capture. Rules then come to protect incumbents rather than consumers. Remedies include cooling off periods before officials may take private sector jobs, although such measures can only partly offset the structural asymmetry of information." },
  { id: 'cz116', diff: 2, topic: 'Supply chains and ethics', text: "Many clothes and electronic products are made in countries where wages are low and safety rules are weak. When a factory collapsed in Bangladesh in the year two thousand thirteen, killing more than a thousand workers, consumers around the world began to ask who was responsible. Many brands have since agreed to inspect their suppliers more carefully. Customers can also help by choosing companies that publish information about how and where their goods are made." },
  { id: 'cz117', diff: 3, topic: 'Auditor independence', text: "An audit is valuable only if investors believe that the auditor is independent from the company being examined. Yet auditors are paid by the very firms they scrutinize, and many also sell lucrative consulting services to them. This arrangement creates a temptation to overlook problems in order to keep a client. Following several corporate scandals, many jurisdictions restricted the non audit services that auditors may provide and required the periodic rotation of audit firms." },
  { id: 'cz118', diff: 4, topic: 'Shareholder versus stakeholder', text: "The proposition that managers should maximize shareholder value has dominated corporate thinking for decades, and its defenders insist that it offers a clear criterion by which executives can be held accountable. Opponents reply that a narrow fixation on quarterly returns encourages neglect of employees, innovation, and the environment, ultimately destroying value. The disagreement is less stark than rhetoric suggests, since even proponents of the stakeholder view concede that firms unable to earn adequate returns will not survive to serve anyone." },
  { id: 'cz119', diff: 4, topic: 'Greenwashing', text: "As consumers and investors have grown more sensitive to environmental concerns, some companies have been tempted to exaggerate their ecological credentials, a practice known as greenwashing. Vague claims such as natural or eco friendly are seldom verifiable, and selective disclosure can create an impression of virtue that operations belie. Regulators in several jurisdictions are tightening standards for environmental claims, while analysts increasingly demand audited data on emissions rather than glossy narratives from corporate communications departments." },
  { id: 'cz120', diff: 4, topic: 'Currency unions', text: "Adopting a common currency removes exchange rate risk and lowers the cost of trade among member states, but it also deprives each government of an independent monetary policy. A country hit by a downturn can no longer devalue its currency to regain competitiveness, so adjustment must occur through unemployment, wage cuts, or transfers from wealthier partners. Whether such unions endure depends on the willingness of the stronger members to accept this solidarity, which explains why the euro crisis proved so politically explosive." }
  );
})();
